        "<p>In the dense cores of evolved stars, the mechanical support of the stellar envelope is governed by the quantum mechanics of degenerate matter, which determines the maximum stable mass of the compact remnant. The mechanical balance in these degenerate environments is defined by <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/stability-limit\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>The Stability Limit of Degenerate Stars</strong></a>, which sets the Chandrasekhar limit for white dwarfs supported by electron degeneracy pressure. If a white dwarf accretes mass from a companion star and exceeds this stability boundary, it undergoes a thermonuclear runaway, producing a Type Ia supernova. Because these explosions occur at a fixed mass threshold, they release a highly uniform absolute luminosity, allowing them to serve as standard candles for distance measurements. By modeling these degenerate cores, stellar astrophysicists can predict the resulting peak absolute luminosity, showing how quantum degeneracy limits dictate the global brightness of supernovae.</p>"
        "<p>The quantitative mathematical representation of this stellar brightness relation is formulated by the inverse-square law. This fundamental geometric formula maps the observed apparent brightness directly to the absolute luminosity and the cosmological distance:</p>"
        "<div class=\"math-display\" style=\"text-align: center; margin: 25px 0;\">\\[ F = \\frac{L}{4\\pi d_L^2} \\]</div>"
        "<p>This geometric equation defines the observed apparent flux \\( F \\) as a function of the absolute luminosity \\( L \\) and the luminosity distance \\( d_L \\), where the factor \\( 4\\pi d_L^2 \\) represents the area of a sphere of radius \\( d_L \\) in expanding spacetime. The macroscopic limiting case of this flux-luminosity relation occurs in the limit of exceptionally small distances, or as the expansion rate of the universe approaches zero. Under this local, static limit, where \\( d_L \\) contracts smoothly to the classical Euclidean distance \\( d \\), the complex cosmological factors that account for redshift and time dilation decouple completely. Consequently, the highly non-local and cosmological features of the luminosity distance contract to the standard flat-space inverse-square law of classical photometry, \\( F = L/4\\pi d^2 \\). This transition mathematically guarantees that the classical Euclidean geometry is recovered without loss of consistency, ensuring that the flux-luminosity relationship remains compatible with classical flat-space electromagnetism. Under this continuous reduction, the cosmological distance equations contract into stable, flat-space geometric relations.</p>"
